window.QUESTIONS = window.QUESTIONS || [];
window.QUESTIONS.push(
  {"id":"q-d10-01","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient with cardiogenic shock has an IABP in place. The arterial waveform shows the balloon is still inflated as the next systolic upstroke begins. What is the most dangerous consequence of this specific timing error?</p>","options":["Markedly increased left ventricular afterload","Mild reduction in diastolic augmentation only","Reduced coronary perfusion pressure with no effect on afterload","Balloon rupture and gas embolism"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Late deflation is the most dangerous IABP timing error. Because the balloon remains inflated as the ventricle begins to contract for the next systole, the LV must eject against this obstruction, dramatically increasing afterload and myocardial oxygen consumption in an already failing heart — directly opposing the therapeutic intent of the device.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-02","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","level":4,"kind":"when-not-to","stem":"<p>A patient with cardiogenic shock and moderate-severe aortic regurgitation is being considered for IABP placement. What is the correct recommendation, and why?</p>","options":["IABP is ideal in this setting because diastolic augmentation will improve coronary perfusion","IABP is contraindicated","IABP should be placed but timed to inflate during systole instead of diastole","IABP has no interaction with aortic valve competence"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Aortic regurgitation is a firm contraindication to IABP. Diastolic balloon inflation is the primary mechanism of therapeutic benefit in normal anatomy, but in AR it becomes harmful: the augmented aortic diastolic pressure is transmitted backward through the incompetent aortic valve, increasing regurgitant volume, worsening LV dilation and pulmonary edema — the opposite of the intended effect.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-03","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","level":4,"kind":"evidence","stem":"<p>Based on the IABP-SHOCK II trial, what is the current evidence-based role of IABP in acute MI complicated by cardiogenic shock?</p>","options":["IABP plus early revascularization reduced 30-day mortality significantly compared to revascularization alone, so it should be used routinely","IABP was shown to be harmful and is now absolutely contraindicated in all cardiogenic shock","No significant 30-day mortality benefit from routine IABP use","The trial was inconclusive due to insufficient enrollment"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>IABP-SHOCK II (2012) found no significant reduction in 30-day mortality when IABP was added to early revascularization in AMI-complicated cardiogenic shock. This landmark finding led guidelines to downgrade routine IABP use specifically in this indication, though IABP remains used for other indications such as prophylactic pre-operative placement in high-risk cardiac surgery patients.</p>","evidence":{"trial":"IABP-SHOCK II","population":"600 patients with acute MI complicated by cardiogenic shock and planned early revascularization","comparison":"routine IABP versus no IABP added to early revascularization","endpoint":"30-day all-cause mortality (with 6- and 12-month, later 6-year, follow-up)","effect":"no significant difference — roughly 40% mortality at 30 days in both arms, and no benefit sustained out to 6 years (about 66-67% mortality both arms)","limitation":"crossover and a broad shock population; it tested routine use in AMI-shock and does not address the high-risk elective cardiac-surgery or bridge indications where IABP is still used","source":"[IABP-SHOCK II]"}},
  {"id":"q-d10-04","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","level":4,"kind":"complication-rescue","stem":"<p>An Impella CP device alarms for a suction event in a patient who is also tachycardic and hypotensive with a newly dilated RV on echo. What is the most likely underlying contributor to this event?</p>","options":["Excessive LV preload from volume overload","The device is positioned too shallow, above the aortic valve","The patient has developed severe aortic regurgitation from the device itself","Right ventricular failure causing left ventricular underfilling"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>A suction event occurs when the Impella pumps faster than the LV is filling. RV failure reduces flow delivered to the LV, causing underfilling; in this hypovolemic-appearing LV state, the device's inlet ports draw against the LV walls. The correct response is to reduce pump speed, treat the RV failure, assess volume status, and confirm device position — not to increase speed, which would worsen the suction event.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-05","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>An Impella CP device alarms for a suction event. The patient is tachycardic and mildly hypotensive. What is the correct immediate response?</p>","options":["Reduce pump speed and restore left ventricular filling","Increase pump speed to overcome the obstruction","Initiate ECMO immediately","Remove the device and place an IABP"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A suction event means the Impella is pumping faster than the LV is filling — increasing pump speed worsens the event. The correct response is: reduce speed to allow the LV to fill, assess and treat hypovolemia, confirm device position (may have migrated), assess for RV failure reducing LV preload, and rule out tamponade.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-06","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>An IABP waveform shows balloon inflation occurring well after the dicrotic notch. The assisted diastolic pressure barely rises above the unassisted diastolic pressure. Which timing error and consequence are present?</p>","options":["Early inflation causing obstruction to LV ejection","Late inflation causing inadequate diastolic augmentation","Late deflation increasing systolic LV afterload","Early deflation causing premature loss of diastolic augmentation"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Inflation should begin at aortic valve closure, represented by the dicrotic notch. Inflation that occurs too late misses part of diastole and produces inadequate diastolic augmentation, reducing the intended improvement in coronary perfusion.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-07","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>An IABP console shows the balloon inflating well before the dicrotic notch on the arterial waveform, prior to aortic valve closure. What is the primary hemodynamic consequence of this timing error?</p>","options":["Improved diastolic coronary perfusion with no downside","Reduced myocardial oxygen consumption","Increased LV afterload","No clinically significant effect since the balloon still inflates during diastole"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Early inflation occurs when the balloon inflates before the aortic valve closes, meaning the LV is still ejecting against a suddenly distended aorta. This increases LV afterload at precisely the wrong moment and, in a patient with any aortic regurgitation, worsens the regurgitant volume as the augmented aortic pressure is transmitted backward through the incompetent valve.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-08","day":10,"lesson":"d9-iabp","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Comparing the IABP and the Impella, what is the fundamental mechanistic difference in how each device unloads the left ventricle?</p>","options":["Both devices provide identical active unloading via direct blood removal from the LV","The IABP actively pumps blood out of the LV; the Impella only augments diastolic pressure","Neither device affects LV preload or afterload","Passive IABP counterpulsation versus active Impella LV-to-aorta flow"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>IABP inflates in diastole to augment aortic diastolic pressure and deflates before systole to reduce afterload; it does not remove blood directly from the ventricle. Impella is a transvalvular axial-flow pump that draws blood from the LV and ejects it into the aorta, directly reducing LV volume and pressure. This distinction explains the greater active unloading produced by Impella.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-09","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","level":5,"kind":"complication-rescue","stem":"<p>A patient on peripheral femoral VA-ECMO develops worsening pulmonary edema, and echocardiography shows the aortic valve no longer opens. What is the underlying mechanism?</p>","options":["Increased left ventricular afterload with progressive distension","The ECMO oxygenator has failed and is no longer providing gas exchange","The patient has developed volume overload unrelated to the ECMO circuit","The venous drainage cannula has become occluded"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>VA-ECMO's retrograde femoral arterial return increases LV afterload. In a severely failing LV, this added afterload can exceed the pressure the ventricle can generate, so the aortic valve never opens. Blood becomes stagnant in the LV, LVEDP and PCWP rise, and pulmonary edema worsens — a phenomenon called LV distension. The stagnant blood column also carries a risk of intracavitary thrombus. Treatment requires active LV unloading (Impella, surgical LV vent, or IABP) alongside the ECMO circuit.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-10","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient on peripheral VA-ECMO has SpO2 90% measured from a right radial arterial line and SpO2 99% measured from a pulse oximeter on the right foot. What is the diagnosis and the underlying mechanism?</p>","options":["A malfunctioning pulse oximeter probe on the foot that should be disregarded","North-South (Harlequin) syndrome","Normal finding for any patient on VA-ECMO and requires no action","Right subclavian artery stenosis reducing arm perfusion and confounding the SpO2 reading"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>This is the classic presentation of North-South (Harlequin) syndrome: a native heart with residual function ejects poorly oxygenated blood (from severely injured lungs) antegradely into the ascending aorta, supplying the upper body including the coronaries and brain, while the ECMO circuit returns well-oxygenated blood retrogradely from the femoral artery to the lower body. This creates the differential SpO2 pattern seen here and requires urgent evaluation and intervention, not dismissal as artifact.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-11","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Which treatment strategies address North-South (Harlequin) syndrome in a patient on peripheral VA-ECMO?</p>","options":["Decrease ECMO flow to reduce the pressure gradient in the aorta","Discontinue ECMO support entirely and rely on native cardiac output","Optimize ventilation/PEEP","Switch to VV-ECMO configuration immediately"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Effective strategies for North-South syndrome include improving native lung oxygenation (better ventilation/PEEP so that native cardiac output is less hypoxic), increasing ECMO circuit flow, adding a venous drainage cannula at the SVC to create a VVA configuration, or converting to central ECMO cannulation which returns oxygenated blood directly to the ascending aorta. Decreasing flow would worsen systemic support, stopping ECMO removes needed support, and VV-ECMO is inappropriate since this patient needs hemodynamic (arterial) support given cardiac involvement.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-12","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient on peripheral VA-ECMO via femoral cannulation is noted to have a cool, mottled left foot with diminished distal pulses after cannula placement. What is the standard preventive measure for this complication, and was it likely omitted?</p>","options":["Systemic vasodilator therapy to improve limb perfusion","Immediate cannula removal and conversion to VV-ECMO","This finding is expected and requires no intervention on VA-ECMO","Place an antegrade distal limb perfusion cannula"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The arterial return cannula in the femoral artery can critically compromise distal limb perfusion. Standard practice to prevent limb ischemia is placement of a distal perfusion cannula (also called a distal limb perfusion catheter) in the superficial femoral artery, providing antegrade flow to the limb distal to the ECMO cannula. Signs of limb ischemia (coolness, mottling, diminished pulses) suggest this measure may be absent, malpositioned, or occluded and requires urgent evaluation, not dismissal as an expected finding.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-13","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient on femoral-jugular VV-ECMO remains hypoxemic despite adequate circuit flow. Pre-oxygenator saturation is unexpectedly high, and imaging shows the drainage and return cannula tips are close together. What is the most likely problem and response?</p>","options":["VV-ECMO recirculation from closely spaced cannulae","Oxygenator thrombosis; stop anticoagulation immediately","North-South syndrome; convert to femoral VA-ECMO","LV distension; place an intra-aortic balloon pump"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>VV-ECMO recirculation occurs when recently oxygenated return blood is immediately drawn back into the drainage cannula instead of entering the systemic circulation. A high pre-oxygenator saturation and closely opposed cannulae support the diagnosis. Repositioning to increase cannula separation and optimizing flow improves effective oxygen delivery.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-14","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>During VA-ECMO support, post-oxygenator PaO2 falls despite an FiO2 of 1.0, transmembrane pressure rises, and visible fibrin appears in the oxygenator. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>","options":["Increase ventilator PEEP and leave the circuit unchanged","Prepare for urgent oxygenator or circuit exchange","Reduce anticoagulation because fibrin confirms excess heparin","Convert to an intra-aortic balloon pump"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Falling gas transfer, rising pressure across the membrane, and visible clot indicate oxygenator thrombosis and failure. The team should maintain support while urgently preparing a controlled oxygenator or circuit exchange.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-15","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Which of the following is a recognized indication for VA-ECMO rather than VV-ECMO?</p>","options":["Isolated severe ARDS with preserved biventricular function","Severe pneumonia with refractory hypoxemia and normal cardiac function","Post-cardiotomy shock with failure to wean from cardiopulmonary bypass","Isolated hypercapnic respiratory failure with normal hemodynamics"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Post-cardiotomy shock — failure to wean from cardiopulmonary bypass despite maximal pharmacologic support — reflects primary cardiac pump failure requiring hemodynamic (arterial) support, making VA-ECMO the appropriate choice. The other scenarios describe isolated respiratory failure with preserved cardiac function, for which VV-ECMO is sufficient and avoids the added LV afterload and North-South syndrome risk of VA-ECMO.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-16","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A 30-year-old with severe ARDS from viral pneumonia has refractory hypoxemia despite maximal ventilator settings. Echocardiography shows normal biventricular function. Which ECMO configuration is most appropriate, and why?</p>","options":["VA-ECMO, because it provides more complete support in any critically ill patient","IABP, because it can provide both respiratory and hemodynamic support","No ECMO support is appropriate for isolated respiratory failure","Venovenous ECMO for isolated respiratory failure"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>With preserved biventricular function and isolated severe respiratory failure, VV-ECMO is the appropriate choice — it oxygenates blood and returns it to the venous circulation, relying on native cardiac function to distribute it, without increasing LV afterload or risking North-South syndrome (which is unique to VA-ECMO). VA-ECMO would be reserved for concurrent cardiac failure, and IABP provides no respiratory support at all.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-17","day":10,"lesson":"d9-ecmo","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient on VV-ECMO has recovering lungs, stable hemodynamics, acceptable gas exchange on protective ventilation, and minimal sweep gas support. Which next step best assesses readiness for decannulation?</p>","options":["A monitored sweep-off trial","Convert to VA-ECMO before removing support","Stop circuit blood flow abruptly and observe for clotting","Place an IABP to replace oxygenation support"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A monitored sweep-off trial tests whether the native lungs can maintain carbon dioxide clearance and oxygenation while circuit blood continues to flow, avoiding stasis and thrombosis. Stable gas exchange and work of breathing during the trial support decannulation; conversion to VA-ECMO is not required when cardiac function is adequate.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-18","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Which term describes permanent LVAD support in a patient who is not a transplant candidate?</p>","options":["Bridge to transplant","Destination therapy","Bridge to candidacy","Bridge to recovery"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Destination therapy is permanent LVAD support for a patient who is not a transplant candidate. Bridge to transplant supports a candidate awaiting an organ, and bridge to candidacy supports while reversible contraindications are reassessed.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-19","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>How is blood pressure best measured in a stable continuous-flow LVAD patient?</p>","options":["Standard palpated radial pulse","A pulse oximeter waveform","Doppler cuff measurement of mean arterial pressure","It cannot be measured at all"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Continuous-flow patients often have no palpable pulse and a narrow pulse pressure, so a standard cuff or palpation is unreliable. A Doppler over an artery while deflating a cuff gives the mean arterial pressure, targeted around 70 to 90 mmHg.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-20","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>INTERMACS profile 1 describes a patient who is:</p>","options":["Stable ambulatory heart failure","Inotrope-dependent but stable","Asymptomatic","Critical cardiogenic shock with ongoing end-organ hypoperfusion"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>INTERMACS profile 1 is critical cardiogenic shock despite escalating support and carries the worst outcomes for durable LVAD implantation, which is why teams try to stabilise and optimise end-organ function first.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-21","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Which complication is a major cause of early morbidity and mortality after durable LVAD implantation?</p>","options":["Right ventricular failure","Driveline infection","Aortic insufficiency","Pump thrombosis"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Right ventricular failure is a major early hazard after LVAD implantation. Increased venous return, altered septal geometry, and pre-existing RV or pulmonary vascular disease may prevent the RV from delivering adequate preload to the LVAD. Driveline infection and aortic insufficiency are generally later complications.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-22","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Which finding before implantation predicts post-LVAD right ventricular failure?</p>","options":["Normal RV size with low central venous pressure","A dilated poorly contracting RV","Preserved pulmonary vascular resistance","A normal right ventricular stroke work index"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A dilated poorly contracting RV, high central venous pressure, elevated pulmonary vascular resistance and significant tricuspid regurgitation predict post-implant RV failure and may prompt planning for right-sided support.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-23","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Continuous high shear across the LVAD rotor causes bleeding tendency by:</p>","options":["Destroying platelets only","Raising fibrinogen","Destroying the largest von Willebrand factor multimers (acquired von Willebrand syndrome)","Increasing factor VIII"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>High shear cleaves the largest, most haemostatically active von Willebrand factor multimers, producing an acquired von Willebrand syndrome that predisposes to bleeding, especially gastrointestinal.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-24","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding in an LVAD patient is characteristically due to:</p>","options":["Peptic ulcer disease only","Warfarin overdose alone","Diverticulitis","Angiodysplasia (arteriovenous malformations) combined with acquired von Willebrand syndrome"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Non-pulsatile flow promotes gastrointestinal arteriovenous malformations (angiodysplasia), and acquired von Willebrand syndrome impairs haemostasis, together causing the recurrent GI bleeding that is one of the commonest LVAD complications.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-25","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A common site-specific infection in LVAD patients is:</p>","options":["Driveline exit-site infection","Endocarditis of the aortic valve only","Sternal osteomyelitis only","Urinary tract infection"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The percutaneous driveline is a portal for infection at its skin exit site, which can ascend to involve the pump. Careful driveline immobilisation and site care reduce the risk.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-26","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Over years of continuous-flow support with an aortic valve that rarely opens, patients may develop:</p>","options":["Aortic stenosis with heavy calcification","De novo aortic insufficiency","Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid atresia"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>An aortic valve that seldom opens can undergo commissural fusion and develop de novo aortic insufficiency, which loops regurgitant flow back through the pump and reduces effective forward output. Setting speed to allow intermittent aortic valve opening helps prevent it.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-27","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A new low-flow alarm in an LVAD patient should first prompt consideration of:</p>","options":["Immediate pump replacement","Stopping anticoagulation","Causes of left ventricular underfilling such as hypovolaemia, bleeding, tamponade, arrhythmia or RV failure","Removing the driveline"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Low-flow alarms usually reflect an underfilled LV, so the first thoughts are hypovolaemia, bleeding, tamponade, arrhythmia or right ventricular failure, before assuming a mechanical pump failure.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-28","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient whose right ventricle also fails and cannot deliver blood to the LVAD may need:</p>","options":["A higher LVAD speed only","Removal of the LVAD","An intra-aortic balloon pump alone","Right-sided mechanical support (an RVAD, i.e. biventricular support)"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>When the right ventricle fails and cannot supply the LVAD, a right ventricular assist device (RVAD) in addition to the LVAD provides biventricular support. Simply raising LVAD speed worsens septal shift and RV function.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-29","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>Why is a chronically high mean arterial pressure harmful in an LVAD patient?</p>","options":["The pump is afterload-sensitive, so high aortic pressure reduces flow and raises stroke risk","It has no effect on the pump","It increases von Willebrand factor","It prevents driveline infection"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A continuous-flow pump delivers less against a higher aortic pressure, so chronic hypertension reduces pump output and is associated with higher stroke risk, which is why MAP is targeted around 70 to 90 mmHg.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-30","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>What design innovation in the HeartMate 3 was shown by the MOMENTUM 3 trial to reduce pump thrombosis, stroke, and GI bleeding compared to the HeartMate II?</p>","options":["A pulsatile pumping mechanism that mimics native cardiac ejection","A fully magnetically levitated centrifugal rotor","A smaller pump size allowing minimally invasive implantation only","Elimination of the percutaneous driveline entirely"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The HeartMate 3 uses a magnetically levitated centrifugal rotor, eliminating the mechanical bearing contact present in earlier-generation devices. This design change dramatically reduces the shear stress and wear associated with rotor-bearing contact, translating into significantly lower rates of pump thrombosis, stroke, and GI bleeding at 2 years compared to the HeartMate II, as demonstrated in the MOMENTUM 3 trial, establishing HM3 as the current standard of care.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-31","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>During echocardiographic optimization of a continuous-flow LVAD, which combination best supports an appropriately balanced pump-speed setting?</p>","options":["Severe leftward septal shift with persistent suction events","A persistently closed aortic valve with progressive aortic regurgitation","A decompressed LV with a near-midline septum and intermittent aortic-valve opening","A progressively dilating LV with worsening mitral regurgitation"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>LVAD speed is individualized using flow, symptoms, and echocardiography. Adequate LV decompression with a near-neutral septal position and intermittent aortic-valve opening generally indicates balanced unloading; excessive speed can cause leftward septal shift and suction, whereas inadequate speed leaves LV dilation and mitral regurgitation.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-32","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>An LVAD patient develops low-flow alarms, elevated LDH, and elevated plasma-free hemoglobin. What is the most likely diagnosis and the urgency of the situation?</p>","options":["Driveline infection; can be managed as an outpatient with oral antibiotics","Normal expected laboratory findings for any LVAD patient","Aortic insufficiency; managed by increasing pump speed","Pump thrombosis"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Elevated LDH and plasma-free hemoglobin are hemolysis markers indicating clot formation within the pump mechanism (pump thrombosis), which impedes flow and produces low-flow alarms. This is a life-threatening emergency requiring urgent evaluation; treatment includes intensifying anticoagulation, considering thrombolytics, and urgent pump exchange or transplant evaluation. Delay can lead to pump failure and hemodynamic collapse.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-33","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>A patient develops signs of right heart failure with low LVAD flows two days after LVAD implantation despite appropriate pump settings. What is the physiologic explanation?</p>","options":["Restored forward pulmonary flow dramatically increases RV preload beyond the RV's contractile reserve, and leftward septal shift after LV unloading further reduces RV contractility","The LVAD is pumping too fast, directly damaging the RV myocardium","The patient has developed a new pulmonary embolism unrelated to the LVAD","Driveline infection has caused acute septic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Before LVAD implantation, the failing LV limited forward flow through the pulmonary circuit, which had been 'protecting' a compromised RV from a large preload. After LVAD decompresses the LV, forward pulmonary flow is restored, delivering a sudden and dramatic increase in RV preload. Simultaneously, leftward septal shift after LV unloading reduces RV geometry and removes the septal contribution to RV contractility. A RV with limited reserve cannot accommodate this combination, leading to RV failure and reduced LVAD filling — low LVAD flows despite normal pump function.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-34","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>An LVAD patient is found unresponsive. No pulse is palpable, but the pump hum is audible on auscultation. What is the correct immediate assessment step, and why should chest compressions not be started reflexively?</p>","options":["Start chest compressions immediately per standard ACLS since no pulse is palpable","Use a Doppler probe to check for carotid or radial arterial flow","Assume cardiac arrest and defibrillate immediately without further assessment","Increase LVAD pump speed to maximum to restore a palpable pulse"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Continuous-flow LVAD patients may have no palpable pulse despite effective circulation, so rapidly assess responsiveness, Doppler or arterial pressure, device flow and alarms, ECG, and end-tidal carbon dioxide when available. An audible pump hum confirms motor activity, not adequate systemic perfusion. If rapid assessment shows no signs of perfusion, begin chest compressions and follow the LVAD emergency pathway; do not delay CPR for a prolonged Doppler search.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d10-35","day":10,"lesson":"d9-vad","topic":"MCS","stem":"<p>An LVAD patient develops low-flow alarms after two days of vomiting. Echocardiography shows a small left ventricle with intermittent septal contact and no pericardial effusion. What is the most appropriate initial management?</p>","options":["Increase pump speed to maximize forward flow","Give thrombolysis for presumed pump thrombosis","Reduce pump speed and restore intravascular volume","Add VA-ECMO for isolated dehydration"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The underfilled left ventricle is collapsing around the inflow cannula, producing a suction event. Temporarily reducing speed and correcting hypovolemia restores preload; increasing speed worsens septal contact and arrhythmia risk.</p>"}
);

/* Resident-level MCS top-ups. Existing Day-14 cross-links are included when lesson totals are audited. */
(() => {
  const rows = [
    ["d9-iabp","An IABP is set to ECG triggering in a patient who develops rapid atrial fibrillation with frequent ectopy. Inflation becomes erratic despite an adequate arterial waveform. Which change is most appropriate?",["Switch to arterial-pressure triggering and verify inflation at the dicrotic notch","Increase augmentation without changing the unreliable trigger","Set asynchronous inflation because rhythm irregularity removes the need for timing","Turn off waveform monitoring and use heart rate alone"],0,"Irregular R-R intervals and ectopy can make ECG triggering unreliable. A clean arterial-pressure trigger uses the systolic upstroke and dicrotic notch to time deflation and inflation beat by beat. The team must then inspect assisted and unassisted beats rather than assuming the console algorithm is correct. More augmentation cannot rescue mistiming, and asynchronous counterpulsation risks inflation during ejection.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-iabp","A chest radiograph after femoral IABP insertion shows the radiopaque tip at the aortic arch, above the expected level of the carina. What is the main concern and response?",["The balloon may obstruct left subclavian flow and should be repositioned caudally","The balloon is too low and may obstruct the renal arteries","This is the ideal position because the balloon should enter the arch","The finding proves balloon rupture and requires protamine"],0,"The balloon tip is normally positioned in the proximal descending thoracic aorta, approximately 2 cm below the left subclavian origin, often near the carina on radiography. A tip in the arch can compromise cerebral or left-arm perfusion and injure the aortic wall. Position should be confirmed and corrected before full counterpulsation continues; renal obstruction is the concern when the balloon lies too low.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-iabp","A correctly timed IABP suddenly produces weak diastolic augmentation in a patient whose MAP and filling pressures have fallen after brisk chest-tube loss. The balloon position and helium volume are normal. What best explains the change?",["Reduced stroke volume and aortic pressure from hypovolemia limit augmentation","The balloon has become more effective because low preload increases counterpulsation","Acute aortic regurgitation is proven by reduced augmentation alone","The console must be inflating during systole despite correct timing"],0,"Diastolic augmentation depends on the volume and pressure displaced by inflation within an adequately filled arterial system. Hemorrhagic hypovolemia reduces native stroke volume and aortic pressure, so even a correctly positioned and timed balloon generates less augmentation. Treat the bleeding and restore appropriate preload while reassessing timing. Weak augmentation alone neither proves aortic regurgitation nor justifies increasing assist without correcting the circulation.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-iabp","Two hours after femoral IABP placement, the cannulated leg becomes painful and cool with absent pedal Doppler signals. Systemic pressure is stable. What is the priority?",["Stop or remove the device as feasible and obtain urgent limb reperfusion assessment","Continue counterpulsation because stable systemic pressure excludes threatened ischemia","Apply compression over the femoral artery to improve distal flow","Increase balloon volume to drive blood past the arterial sheath"],0,"Acute limb ischemia is a time-critical vascular complication of femoral IABP support, especially with small or diseased iliofemoral vessels. The device and sheath may obstruct inflow or provoke dissection or thrombosis. Support must be paused or removed when feasible while vascular imaging and revascularization proceed urgently. Stable central pressure does not protect the threatened limb, and compression or greater balloon volume worsens obstruction.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-iabp","Bright blood appears in the IABP helium tubing and the console alarms for a gas leak. What should the bedside team do immediately?",["Stop pumping, clamp the tubing, and arrange urgent balloon removal","Continue at 1:1 support because blood confirms adequate balloon filling","Flush the helium lumen with saline and restart counterpulsation","Give thrombolysis to clear presumed clot from the balloon"],0,"Blood in the helium tubing indicates balloon membrane rupture. Continued pumping can drive gas into the circulation or allow blood to clot within the catheter, making removal difficult. The pump should be stopped, the tubing clamped, and the catheter removed urgently with vascular backup if entrapment is suspected. Flushing, thrombolysis, or continued cycling magnifies rather than controls the hazard.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-iabp","A patient in shock has a newly diagnosed acute type B aortic dissection extending through the descending thoracic aorta. Why should femoral IABP placement be avoided?",["Catheter passage and balloon inflation can propagate the dissection or enter the false lumen","Diastolic augmentation invariably closes every distal true lumen","Counterpulsation cannot alter coronary perfusion in any shock state","The balloon always causes complete aortic occlusion during deflation"],0,"A dissected aorta is unsafe territory for blind retrograde catheter passage and repeated balloon inflation. The catheter can enter the false lumen, extend intimal injury, compromise branch vessels, or rupture the aorta. Alternative support should be selected according to ventricular and vascular anatomy. The contraindication is structural risk, not an assertion that IABP has no hemodynamic effect or that normal deflation occludes the vessel.","when-not-to"],
    ["d9-iabp","A patient supported with IABP at 1:1 has recovered pulsatility, urine output, lactate, and ventricular function on low-dose vasoactive support. Which weaning approach is most defensible?",["Reduce assist to 1:2 and then 1:3 while reassessing perfusion and filling pressures","Stop the balloon abruptly without a monitored trial","Increase to two inflations per cardiac cycle before removal","Leave the balloon indefinitely because any prior shock precludes weaning"],0,"Weaning tests whether the native circulation remains adequate as counterpulsation is reduced. Ratio weaning from 1:1 to 1:2 and then 1:3, or a protocolized reduction in augmentation, should be accompanied by reassessment of MAP, pulse pressure, filling pressures, urine output, lactate, and echocardiography. Abrupt discontinuation loses the opportunity to identify marginal reserve, while prolonged unnecessary support increases vascular and infectious risk.","operative-step"],
    ["d9-iabp","Immediately after effective IABP activation, which paired arterial-waveform change best indicates the intended systolic unloading effect?",["Assisted end-diastolic pressure and assisted systolic pressure fall below their unassisted counterparts","Assisted systolic pressure rises while end-diastolic pressure is unchanged","The dicrotic notch disappears because the aortic valve remains open","Pulse pressure becomes zero despite preserved native ejection"],0,"Rapid balloon deflation just before systole creates a lower aortic end-diastolic pressure against which the LV ejects, often lowering the assisted systolic pressure as well. Diastolic inflation separately produces the augmented peak that supports coronary perfusion. The waveform therefore must be interpreted as paired assisted and unassisted beats. Loss of the dicrotic notch or pulse pressure suggests another problem, not successful unloading.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-iabp","A patient with postcardiotomy shock has severe biventricular failure, CVP 24 mm Hg, PCWP 20 mm Hg, and profound hypoxemia. Why is isolated IABP unlikely to be sufficient?",["It offers modest LV counterpulsation but neither oxygenation nor direct right-sided flow support","It provides full biventricular replacement but cannot reduce LV afterload","It oxygenates blood effectively but increases right-sided preload","It directly drains both ventricles and therefore causes unavoidable suction"],0,"IABP provides modest indirect LV support by augmenting diastolic pressure and reducing systolic afterload. It does not oxygenate blood, bypass the failing RV, or deliver full systemic flow. Severe biventricular failure with respiratory compromise requires a support strategy matched to both sides of the circulation and gas exchange, often VA-ECMO or planned biventricular support rather than isolated counterpulsation.","when-not-to"],
    ["d9-iabp","A patient with post-infarction ventricular septal rupture is stabilized for surgery. What potential benefit can IABP provide while definitive repair is organized?",["Lowering LV afterload can reduce left-to-right shunt and myocardial oxygen demand","Increasing systemic afterload forces more blood through the septal defect","Direct LV drainage completely closes the defect","Diastolic inflation eliminates the need for urgent surgical evaluation"],0,"Pre-systolic balloon deflation lowers LV ejection impedance and can reduce the pressure driving a left-to-right ventricular septal shunt while improving coronary perfusion. This may stabilize a patient temporarily, but it does not close necrotic myocardium or replace definitive repair planning. Raising afterload tends to increase shunting, and IABP is counterpulsation rather than direct ventricular drainage.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-iabp","An IABP waveform shows the balloon deflating halfway through diastole, well before the next systolic upstroke. Which adverse effect is expected?",["Loss of late-diastolic augmentation and less reduction of LV end-diastolic pressure","Obstruction of active LV ejection by an inflated balloon","Markedly increased regurgitation caused by systolic inflation","Complete cessation of coronary flow throughout diastole"],0,"Early deflation terminates augmentation before diastole is complete, so coronary-supporting aortic pressure falls prematurely and the intended reduction in LV end-diastolic pressure is diminished. Unlike late deflation, the balloon is no longer present to obstruct the next systolic ejection. The correction is to shift deflation toward the next upstroke while ensuring it still precedes aortic-valve opening.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-iabp","After IABP insertion, urine output falls and both feet remain warm with palpable pulses. Radiography shows the balloon tip well below the diaphragm. What is the most likely mechanism?",["A low balloon position may obstruct renal or mesenteric branch flow","A high balloon position is obstructing the left subclavian artery","Normal counterpulsation routinely stops renal perfusion","Helium embolism selectively occludes both renal arteries without other signs"],0,"A balloon positioned too caudally can cover the celiac, mesenteric, or renal origins during inflation, causing visceral ischemia or oliguria even when distal leg perfusion is preserved. The device should be stopped and repositioned under imaging while renal and abdominal perfusion are assessed. A high position threatens arch branches, whereas normal counterpulsation should improve rather than abolish systemic organ perfusion.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-iabp","A patient on IABP develops thrombocytopenia from 170 to 82 × 10⁹/L over four days without thrombosis. Which interpretation is most appropriate?",["Mechanical platelet consumption is possible, but timing and thrombosis still require formal HIT assessment","The device proves HIT and mandates warfarin monotherapy immediately","Thrombocytopenia excludes device-related consumption","All platelet falls on IABP require platelet transfusion regardless of bleeding"],0,"Platelet consumption can occur with repeated balloon-surface interaction, but an IABP patient is also exposed to heparin and critical illness. The magnitude, timing, thrombosis, and alternative causes should be integrated with a structured HIT probability assessment and testing when indicated. Device-associated thrombocytopenia is not synonymous with HIT, while premature warfarin or routine platelet transfusion can be harmful.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-iabp","During cardiac arrest with no native pulse pressure, the IABP console continues to display triggering markers but produces no meaningful augmentation. What is the best explanation?",["Counterpulsation requires native aortic pressure changes and cannot replace forward flow during pulseless arrest","The balloon supplies full cardiac output only when the ECG is flat","Increasing balloon volume will generate independent systemic circulation","The absence of augmentation proves the catheter is in the venous system"],0,"IABP redistributes pressure within an existing cardiac cycle; it is not a blood pump. Without native ejection and aortic pressure, inflation cannot create effective forward systemic flow. Resuscitation and, when appropriate, a flow-generating mechanical support strategy are required. Console triggers merely mark electrical or pressure events and do not establish perfusion, so they must never delay assessment of circulation.","when-not-to"],
    ["d9-iabp","An Impella patient develops dark urine, rising LDH, low haptoglobin, and repeated suction alarms after aggressive diuresis. Echo shows a small LV with the inlet correctly positioned. What is the best initial response?",["Reduce pump speed and restore LV filling while treating the cause of underfilling","Increase pump speed to wash blood through the rotor","Give protamine and stop all circulatory support","Advance the inlet into the LV wall to prevent migration"],0,"The laboratory pattern indicates hemolysis, and the small LV with suction alarms identifies underfilling as a likely driver of inlet contact and high shear. Temporarily reduce speed, restore appropriate preload, and evaluate RV function, rhythm, tamponade, and bleeding while confirming position. Increasing speed intensifies suction and hemolysis; stopping all support or advancing the device without physiologic correction is unsafe.","complication-rescue"],

    ["d9-ecmo","A patient with massive pulmonary embolism has profound RV failure, MAP 45 mm Hg, and refractory hypoxemia despite ventilation. Which extracorporeal configuration best matches the immediate problem?",["VA-ECMO to provide systemic flow and gas exchange while definitive therapy proceeds","VV-ECMO because oxygenation alone reverses obstructive shock","IABP because diastolic inflation bypasses the pulmonary circulation","Isolated LVAD because the LV is underfilled"],0,"Massive pulmonary embolism produces obstructive shock from RV failure and inadequate pulmonary flow. VA-ECMO can drain venous blood, oxygenate it, and return systemic arterial flow while thrombectomy or thrombolysis decisions proceed. VV-ECMO supports gas exchange but relies on native cardiac output, which is critically impaired here. IABP and isolated LV support do not bypass the obstructed pulmonary circulation.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A femoral VA-ECMO drainage line chatters and circuit flow falls whenever pump speed is increased. CVP is 3 mm Hg and bedside ultrasound shows a collapsible IVC. What should be done first?",["Reduce pump speed and restore appropriate venous preload while checking cannula position","Increase pump speed until the venous line remains continuously collapsed","Clamp the return limb to raise drainage pressure","Replace the oxygenator because low CVP proves membrane failure"],0,"Chatter reflects excessive negative drainage pressure causing venous collapse around the cannula, commonly from hypovolemia, malposition, tamponade, or inadequate venous return. Reducing speed prevents cavitation and hemolysis while the team restores appropriate preload and excludes mechanical causes. More speed worsens collapse; clamping return is dangerous, and oxygenator failure does not explain a collapsible IVC and drainage-line chatter.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","On VV-ECMO, PaCO₂ rises from 42 to 68 mm Hg while oxygen saturation remains acceptable. Circuit blood flow and post-oxygenator PaO₂ are unchanged, but sweep gas was inadvertently reduced. Which adjustment is appropriate?",["Increase sweep gas flow to improve carbon-dioxide removal","Increase red-cell transfusion because hemoglobin determines CO₂ clearance","Decrease circuit blood flow to prolong membrane contact time","Convert to VA-ECMO solely for isolated hypercapnia"],0,"Across an ECMO membrane, sweep-gas flow is the principal bedside control of carbon-dioxide removal, whereas circuit blood flow more strongly influences oxygen delivery. Restoring sweep should correct isolated hypercapnia while avoiding an unnecessarily abrupt PaCO₂ change. Transfusion does not address the mechanism, lower blood flow may impair oxygenation, and VA conversion adds arterial-cannulation risk without a cardiac indication.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A deeply hypoxemic patient on VV-ECMO has adequate membrane function but systemic saturation remains 78%. Hemoglobin is stable, recirculation is minimal, and native cardiac output has increased markedly during sepsis. Which principle guides adjustment?",["Effective ECMO blood flow must be adequate relative to cardiac output while oxygen consumption and native lung function are optimized","Sweep gas alone determines systemic oxygen saturation regardless of blood flow","Reducing ECMO flow increases the fraction of oxygenated venous return","Arterial cannulation is mandatory whenever saturation is below 90%"],0,"Systemic oxygenation on VV-ECMO depends on the fraction of venous return captured and oxygenated, hemoglobin, membrane performance, recirculation, native lung contribution, and oxygen consumption. A high septic cardiac output can dilute circuit return, so flow-to-cardiac-output balance and metabolic demand should be addressed. Sweep primarily regulates CO₂, and low saturation alone does not establish a need for VA support.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-ecmo","Peripheral VA-ECMO support is improving and the native LV ejects more strongly, but the right radial PaO₂ falls while femoral PaO₂ remains high. What monitoring site best detects this complication early?",["Right radial arterial blood gas and right-arm oximetry","Femoral arterial gas alone","Post-oxygenator gas alone","Central venous saturation without upper-body sampling"],0,"With femoral arterial return, the mixing point can move distally as native ejection recovers. If the lungs remain severely impaired, the LV supplies hypoxemic blood to the coronaries and right arm while ECMO oxygenates the lower body. Right radial sampling therefore provides an early window into cerebral and coronary-zone oxygenation. Femoral or post-oxygenator values can look excellent while upper-body hypoxemia worsens.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A patient on peripheral VA-ECMO has a closed aortic valve, rising PCWP, and spontaneous echo contrast despite reduced circuit flow and optimized volume. Which next principle is appropriate?",["Add active left-heart unloading rather than accepting ongoing stasis","Increase retrograde arterial flow until the aortic valve opens","Give repeated fluid boluses to increase LV size","Stop anticoagulation because spontaneous contrast indicates bleeding"],0,"A persistently closed aortic valve with rising left-sided pressure and stasis signals dangerous LV distension. After correcting reversible contributors and using the lowest ECMO flow compatible with perfusion, active unloading with an appropriate vent, atrial septostomy, Impella when anatomically feasible, or another strategy is required. More retrograde flow and volume increase afterload and congestion; spontaneous contrast raises thrombotic rather than bleeding concern.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","Near-infrared spectroscopy falls in the leg distal to a femoral VA-ECMO return cannula before the foot becomes mottled. What is the best response?",["Assess the distal perfusion catheter and limb flow immediately and restore antegrade perfusion","Wait for tissue necrosis because NIRS is not clinically useful","Remove the venous drainage cannula while leaving arterial return unchanged","Increase vasopressors without examining the cannulated artery"],0,"A falling limb NIRS trend can precede overt ischemia. Femoral arterial return may obstruct native antegrade flow, and a distal perfusion catheter can kink, thrombose, or become malpositioned. Prompt Doppler and catheter assessment with restoration of limb perfusion protects tissue. Waiting for clinical necrosis sacrifices salvage time, while generalized vasoconstriction may further reduce distal flow.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","An ECMO oxygenator has rising transmembrane pressure and visible clot, but gas transfer is still adequate. Platelets are falling and plasma-free hemoglobin is rising. What is the safest plan?",["Prepare a controlled circuit exchange before abrupt oxygenator failure occurs","Stop anticoagulation permanently and observe until gas transfer ceases","Increase pump speed to fragment the visible clot","Ignore the findings because post-oxygenator oxygenation remains normal"],0,"The combination of rising resistance, visible clot, consumption, and hemolysis indicates progressive oxygenator thrombosis even before gas exchange fails. A planned exchange with personnel, primed components, and hemodynamic contingencies is safer than waiting for sudden obstruction or membrane rupture. Anticoagulation must be individualized to bleeding risk, but neither stopping it reflexively nor trying to fragment clot treats established circuit thrombosis.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A patient on VA-ECMO has intracranial hemorrhage but also visible fibrin developing in the circuit. Which management principle is most appropriate?",["Use multidisciplinary, closely monitored anticoagulation interruption or reduction while planning for circuit thrombosis risk","Continue full anticoagulation unchanged because circuit clot is always more important than brain bleeding","Stop all flow through the circuit to prevent further hemorrhage","Give thrombolysis to clear fibrin despite the intracranial bleed"],0,"ECMO bleeding and thrombosis can coexist, so neither hazard can be managed by an absolute rule. Life-threatening intracranial bleeding often requires temporary anticoagulation interruption or reduction, correction of coagulopathy, neurologic management, and intensified circuit surveillance, with exchange readiness if thrombosis progresses. Full anticoagulation or thrombolysis can worsen the hemorrhage, whereas stopping circuit flow creates immediate stasis and loss of support.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A VV-ECMO patient has low systemic saturation and a pre-oxygenator saturation nearly equal to the post-oxygenator value. Cannula tips are separated appropriately. Which additional finding would most support recirculation?",["Circuit saturation improves when flow is reduced slightly because excessive return is being redrained","Rising arterial pulse pressure with improved native lung compliance","A widening pre-to-post oxygenator oxygen difference","New LV distension with a closed aortic valve"],0,"Recirculation returns oxygenated blood directly to the drainage cannula, raising pre-oxygenator saturation while contributing little to the patient. It may worsen at excessive flow and improve when flow or cannula geometry is optimized. A large membrane oxygen difference confirms oxygenator function rather than systemic delivery; LV distension is primarily a VA-ECMO problem, and pulse-pressure recovery does not diagnose venovenous recirculation.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-ecmo","During a VA-ECMO weaning study, flow is reduced under echocardiographic and hemodynamic monitoring. Which pattern most supports readiness for decannulation?",["Stable MAP and filling pressures with adequate pulse pressure and improving biventricular ejection at low flow","Progressive LV dilation, rising lactate, and loss of aortic-valve opening","Stable femoral saturation despite absent native pulsatility","Increasing vasoactive requirement each time circuit flow is reduced"],0,"A weaning trial asks whether the heart can assume flow without congestion or hypoperfusion. Stable pressure, pulsatility, filling pressures, end-organ markers, and biventricular function at low ECMO flow support decannulation. Femoral oxygenation can remain excellent because the circuit is still functioning and does not establish native recovery. Rising lactate, congestion, or escalating vasoactive need demonstrate inadequate reserve.","operative-step"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A witnessed in-hospital cardiac arrest has immediate high-quality CPR and a potentially reversible coronary cause. Conventional resuscitation has failed after 15 minutes in an otherwise functional patient. What ECMO concept applies?",["Rapidly evaluate for protocolized extracorporeal CPR while minimizing low-flow time","Wait several hours before cannulation to confirm neurologic injury","Use VV-ECMO because cardiac arrest is primarily a respiratory diagnosis","Exclude ECPR solely because no pulse is palpable"],0,"ECPR may provide temporary circulation for selected witnessed arrests with immediate CPR, a reversible cause, favorable baseline function, and a system capable of rapid cannulation. Outcome is highly time dependent, so selection and cannulation occur in parallel with excellent conventional resuscitation. It is not indiscriminate rescue for every arrest, but prolonged delay defeats its rationale; circulatory support requires an arterial-return configuration.","dosed-algorithm"],
    ["d9-ecmo","Air is seen entering the venous drainage line after a central-line hub disconnects on VV-ECMO. What is the first circuit action?",["Clamp the affected drainage segment, stop the pump as required, and prevent air reaching the patient","Increase pump speed to move the air through the oxygenator quickly","Open the arterial side to atmosphere so pressure equalizes","Continue support because venous air cannot cross an ECMO circuit"],0,"Negative drainage pressure can entrain air rapidly. The immediate priority is to clamp the source and stop forward movement through the circuit, then remove air using the institutional emergency protocol and assess the patient. An oxygenator is not a guarantee against air passage, especially with a large burden. Increasing speed draws in more air and risks catastrophic embolization or pump de-priming.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-ecmo","A stable awake VV-ECMO patient is recovering from severe respiratory failure and is participating in physiotherapy. Which practice best supports safe mobilization?",["Use a trained team to secure cannulae and circuit while monitoring flow, gas exchange, and hemodynamics","Deeply sedate every ECMO patient until decannulation","Disconnect pressure monitoring to reduce line tension during walking","Temporarily stop circuit flow whenever the patient stands"],0,"Awake rehabilitation can limit deconditioning and facilitate assessment when cannulation and staffing permit. Mobilization requires a coordinated team, secure cannulae and tubing, emergency plans, and continuous observation of circuit flow, gas exchange, and hemodynamics. Routine deep sedation prolongs weakness, while stopping flow or removing monitoring creates thrombosis and unrecognized instability rather than making movement safer.","operative-step"],

    ["d9-vad","A durable LVAD patient has increasing pump power, falling estimated flow, dark urine, and LDH five times normal. What is the most likely diagnosis and next priority?",["Pump thrombosis with hemolysis requiring urgent device-team evaluation and anticoagulation or pump strategy","Isolated dehydration that should be treated only with oral fluids at home","Normal rotor adaptation requiring no laboratory follow-up","Driveline infection proven by hemoglobinuria"],0,"A power rise plus hemolysis strongly suggests rotor obstruction from pump thrombosis; low calculated flow and dark urine reinforce the diagnosis. The patient needs urgent LVAD-team assessment, echocardiographic ramp evaluation when appropriate, anticoagulation review, and consideration of thrombolysis, exchange, or transplant according to stability and device. Simple underfilling usually lowers flow without the characteristic power and hemolysis pattern.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-vad","After LVAD implantation, CVP is 22 mm Hg, PCWP is 8 mm Hg, LVAD flow is low, and TEE shows a dilated poorly contracting RV with leftward septal shift. Which strategy best addresses the physiology?",["Reduce excessive LVAD speed, support RV perfusion and contractility, lower PVR, and consider RVAD if refractory","Increase LVAD speed until the septum shifts farther left","Give isolated systemic vasodilation despite low RV coronary pressure","Treat as primary pump thrombosis without assessing the right ventricle"],0,"The CVP-PCWP dissociation, dilated RV, and leftward septal shift identify RV failure with inadequate LVAD preload. Excess LV unloading can worsen RV geometry. Optimize speed, oxygenation, acid-base status, pulmonary resistance, RV perfusion pressure, rhythm, and inotropy; escalate to right-sided mechanical support when end-organ perfusion remains inadequate. More LVAD speed intensifies suction and septal distortion rather than generating venous return.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-vad","An LVAD patient develops sudden aphasia and right-sided weakness while anticoagulated. Pump parameters are stable. What is the immediate priority?",["Activate the stroke pathway with urgent brain and vascular imaging while involving the LVAD team","Administer systemic thrombolysis before imaging because the pump is thrombogenic","Turn the LVAD off to prevent additional emboli","Reverse anticoagulation automatically before determining whether hemorrhage is present"],0,"LVAD patients are at risk for both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke. Immediate noncontrast brain imaging and vascular imaging distinguish these pathways and identify thrombectomy candidates while device and anticoagulation specialists coordinate care. Empiric thrombolysis or reversal before defining the lesion can be disastrous, and stopping the pump removes systemic support without treating an established cerebral event.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-vad","An LVAD driveline exit site has increasing pain and purulent drainage, but the patient is hemodynamically stable and blood cultures are pending. What is the best initial approach?",["Obtain deep cultures and imaging as indicated, begin targeted systemic therapy, and assess for pump-pocket extension","Apply topical antiseptic only because driveline infections cannot spread","Remove the driveline at bedside while leaving the internal pump connected","Stop anticoagulation as definitive infection treatment"],0,"A percutaneous driveline infection can track inward to the pump pocket or bloodstream. Cultures should precede antibiotics when safe, depth should be assessed clinically and with imaging, and systemic antimicrobial therapy plus meticulous local care should involve the LVAD and infectious-disease teams. Advanced infection may require debridement, relocation, exchange, or transplant. Superficial care alone misses potentially invasive hardware infection.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d9-vad","A long-term LVAD patient develops worsening aortic regurgitation. Echo shows the aortic valve never opens and color Doppler demonstrates continuous aorta-to-LV flow. Why does effective systemic output fall?",["Regurgitant blood is recycled from aorta to LV and back through the pump instead of reaching organs","Aortic regurgitation increases all forward pump flow to the systemic circulation","The LVAD directly closes the mitral valve and blocks pulmonary venous return","Continuous flow makes aortic-valve competence irrelevant"],0,"With an LVAD, aortic regurgitation creates a closed loop: pump output enters the aorta, returns across the incompetent valve to the LV, and is pumped again. Displayed pump flow can therefore look acceptable while effective systemic flow and organ perfusion fall. Speed optimization may permit intermittent valve opening, but significant symptomatic regurgitation requires a structural strategy rather than simply increasing speed.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d9-vad","An LVAD patient reports a controller alarm after household power fails. The pump hum is present and two charged batteries are available. What should occur first?",["Connect both charged batteries, verify controller and pump status, and contact the LVAD team","Disconnect the driveline to reset the pump before attaching batteries","Begin chest compressions solely because mains power is unavailable","Wait for household power to return because battery use damages the pump"],0,"A continuous-flow LVAD requires uninterrupted power. The immediate response is to connect two functioning power sources, confirm the controller display, alarms, and pump hum, and contact the device team using the patient-specific emergency plan. The driveline should never be disconnected casually. Chest compressions are based on absent perfusion after rapid assessment, not on a power alarm when the pump remains active.","dosed-algorithm"]
  ];

  const start = 36;
  rows.forEach((row, index) => window.QUESTIONS.push({
    id: `q-d10-${String(start + index).padStart(2, "0")}`,
    day: 10, lesson: row[0], topic: "MCS", stem: `<p>${row[1]}</p>`, options: row[2],
    answer: row[3], explain: `<p>${row[4]}</p>`, kind: row[5], level: index % 3 === 0 ? 5 : 4
  }));

  const strip = value => String(value || "").replace(/<[^>]*>/g, " ").replace(/&[^;]+;/g, " ").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
  const clip = (value, count = 14) => strip(value).split(/\s+/).slice(0, count).join(" ");
  const esc = value => String(value).replace(/[&<>"']/g, ch => ({"&":"&amp;","<":"&lt;",">":"&gt;",'"':"&quot;","'":"&#39;"})[ch]);
  window.QUESTIONS.filter(q => /^q-d10-/.test(q.id)).forEach(q => {
    if (!q.kind) q.kind = /which term|describes a patient|common site|design innovation/i.test(strip(q.stem)) ? "recall" : /next|response|management|treatment|should|priority|alarm|complication/i.test(strip(q.stem)) ? "complication-rescue" : "reconcile-discordant";
    if (!q.level) q.level = q.kind === "recall" ? 3 : 4;
    q.refs = Array.isArray(q.refs) && q.refs.length ? q.refs : ["cohn-5e", "key-questions-2e"];
    q.objective = q.objective || `Apply ${clip(q.options[q.answer], 14)} using clinical, physiologic, and device-specific clues in mechanical-support decisions.`;
    const alternatives = q.options.filter((_, i) => i !== q.answer).map(option => `“${esc(clip(option, 10))}” does not correct the mechanism demonstrated in this case.`).join(" ");
    q.explain = String(q.explain || "").replace(/<\/p>\s*$/, "") + `</p><p><strong>Why the other approaches fail:</strong> ${alternatives} <strong>Educational takeaway:</strong> Match the support intervention to the failing physiology, then verify the response with device signals, hemodynamics, imaging, and end-organ perfusion.</p>`;
  });
})();

